  address = {Washington, DC, US},
  doi = {10.1037/0000290-006},
  abstract = {This chapter recounts three types of scholarly work on virtual reality (VR). In line with Fox, Arena, and Bailenson (2009), it reviews research that considers VR as an object, as a method, and as an application. First, the chapter discusses the psychological effects of VR technology as an object, especially the role of presence, immersion, and embodiment. It also touches upon gender differences that emerge when using VR. Second, the chapter synthesizes the publications that address how VR can push social psychology forward. VR technologies allow scholars to set up and replicate experiments that are dangerous, expensive, or impossible in the real world. On top of this, VR allows for the precise capturing of behavioral data. Besides its research application, the chapter lastly discusses real-world applications of VR technology and particularly outlines the use of VR for promoting empathy and facilitating education. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)},
